\begin_layout Section
On the Role of Dependence 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{1,2,\dots,n\}$
\end_inset

 be 
\emph on
completely 
\emph default
dependent stochastic variables,
 by which i mean 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(X_{1}=X_{2}=\cdots=X_{n}\right)=1,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}=\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{1}=X_{1}\in\{0,1\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let the 
\emph on
bias
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
succes probability
\emph default
 of the all the bernoulli random variables be 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}\left(X_{i}=1\right)=\mathbb{P}\left(X_{i}=0\right)=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

).
 Then calculating the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1}$
\end_inset

 (or any 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

) yields 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu\equiv\mathbb{E}\left[X_{1}\right]=\mathbb{P}\left(X_{1}=1\right)=\frac{1}{2},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and thus we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mu-\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right|\mid\in\left\{ \frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and can complete the argument:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\mu-\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right|\right)=\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\frac{1}{2}-X_{1}\right|\right)=\frac{1}{2}\geq\frac{1}{2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
On Confidence Intervals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Part 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Firstly we remember the definition of a confidence interval for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 as the function of the datapoints 
\begin_inset Formula $CI(X_{1},X_{2},\cdots X_{n})$
\end_inset

 fulfilling
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left(\theta\in CI(X_{1},X_{2},\cdots X_{n})\right)\geq1-\alpha,\label{eq:confidence_definitions}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is the significance level.
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=0.02$
\end_inset

 we get the 
\begin_inset Formula $98\%-CI$
\end_inset

 that the assignment revolves around.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\;$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 
\series bold
False
\series default
:
 The confidence does not generally try to encompass the observed variables by some margin (this is also the explanation for question 2).
 It rather uses some bound (for example Hoeffdings bound) to say that with some probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=1-\alpha$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\;$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 
\series bold
False
\series default
:
 See 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\;$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.

\series bold
 False
\series default
:
 By the definition of a confidence interval (i.e.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:confidence_definitions}
\end_layout

\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula $CI$
\end_inset

 is a stochastic variable that depending on the realisation of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{n}$
\end_inset

 and thus is generally not the same between two different draws of data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\;$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.

\series bold
 True
\series default
 (in the case of CI based on Hoeffding bound):
 By the double sided Hoeffding bound we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\right|\leq\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\right)\geq1-\delta.
\]

\end_inset

This is shown by choosing rewriting the parameters and taking the complement of the original event.
 Rewriting the event (by scaling with 
\begin_inset Formula $1/n$
\end_inset

) we obtain the following
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left|\frac{1}{n}\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\frac{1}{n}\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\right|=\left|\hat{\mu}_{n}-\mu\right| & \leq\sqrt{-\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2n^{2}}\ln\left(\frac{\delta}{2}\right)}\iff\\
-\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\leq & \hat{\mu}_{n}-\mu\leq\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\iff
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mu}_{n}+\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\geq\mu\geq\hat{\mu}_{n}-\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\label{eq:CI_interval_hoeffding}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

That is with at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\delta$
\end_inset

 probability the 
\emph on
true
\emph default
 / 
\emph on
population
\emph default
 expected value 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 will fulfill 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:CI_interval_hoeffding}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 which formally mean
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left(\mu\in\left[\hat{\mu}_{n}-\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2n^{2}}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)},\quad\hat{\mu}_{n}+\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2n^{2}}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\right]\right)\geq1-\delta,\label{eq:hoeff_CI}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the definition of the confidence interval (see 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:confidence_definitions}
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 From 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:hoeff_CI}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 we see that decreasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 (for example from 
\begin_inset Formula $0.02$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $0.01$
\end_inset

) implies a larger confidence interval,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{d}{d\delta}\left(\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)\right)=\frac{d}{d\delta}\left(-\ln\delta\right)=-\frac{1}{\delta}<0,
\]

\end_inset

and thus decreasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 has a positive effect on 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln\left(2/\delta\right)$
\end_inset

 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2n^{2}}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However in general we could define some weird confidence interval procedure that fulfills 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:confidence_definitions}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 by setting 
\begin_inset Formula $I=(-\infty,\infty)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta>0.01$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $I=\left[\hat{\mu}_{n}-\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2n^{2}}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)},\quad\hat{\mu}_{n}+\sqrt{\frac{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}{2n^{2}}\ln\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\right]$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\in[0,0.01]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

