\begin_layout Subsection
Convolutional neural networks 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I define the model class as using PyTorch as the assignemnt reccomends.
 I define the two convolutional layers:
 the first takes 3 inputlayers (i.e.
 the RBG values of the pictures) and outputs 64 feature maps.
 The kernel is 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times5$
\end_inset

 so the feature map is now a square with sidelengths both equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $28-5+1=24$
\end_inset

,
 and we apply the ELU activation function.
 This is pooled using a 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 maxpool layer and the dimension is now 
\begin_inset Formula $12\times12$
\end_inset

.
 The second Convolutional layer takes 64 inputs (i.e.
 the maxpooled feature maps of the first block) and outputs 64 feature maps.
 Dimension of each feature map is thus 
\begin_inset Formula $12-5+1=8$
\end_inset

.
 The same pooling is then applied.
 Finally the 
\begin_inset Formula $4\times4\times64=1024$
\end_inset

 values are flattened into a layer fully connected to 43 output neurons.
 The 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
texttt{foward}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 method of the class defines this flow:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
inline false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
class Net(nn.Module):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    def __init__(self):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        super().__init__()
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        self.conv1 = nn.Conv2d(3,
 64,
 kernel_size=5)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        self.pool1 = nn.MaxPool2d(2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        self.conv2 = nn.Conv2d(64,
 64,
 kernel_size=5)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        self.pool2 = nn.MaxPool2d(2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(1024,
 43)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    def forward(self,
 x):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        x = self.pool1(F.elu(self.conv1(x)))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        x = self.pool2(F.elu(self.conv2(x)))
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        x = x.flatten(1)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        x = self.fc2(x)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
        return x
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Augmentation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The training data are first resized to $32
\backslash
times32$.
 Four augmentations are then applied:
 a random rotation between $-5^
\backslash
circ$ and $5^
\backslash
circ$,
 a random $28
\backslash
times28$ crop,
 random brightness and contrast changes,
 and a horizontal flip with probability $0.5$.
 The horizontal flip is conditioned on the label and is only applied to labels $11,12,13,17,18,26,30,$ and $35$.
 These are all signs that are horizontally symetrical (i.e.
 their meaning is preserved under horizontal flipping).
 For the test data,
 only a deterministic center crop is used.
 Finally,
 all images are converted to tensors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As an additional augmentation I use a small random perspective transformation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
image = transforms.RandomPerspective(
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    distortion_scale=0.15,
 p=0.3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
)(image)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This is reasonable for traffic-sign recognition since signs are often photographed from a slight angle rather than perfectly front-on.
 A small perspective distortion can therefore approximate realistic variation in the viewing angle while preserving the class of the sign.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

